does (?i) match?          java.util.regex, anchored
pattern   subject                                        (?i)   (?iu)
k         U+004B LATIN CAPITAL LETTER K                  yes    yes
k         U+212A KELVIN SIGN                             no     yes
s         U+017F LATIN SMALL LETTER LONG S               no     yes
U+00DF    U+1E9E LATIN CAPITAL LETTER SHARP S            no     no
U+1E9E    U+00DF LATIN SMALL LETTER SHARP S              no     yes
ss        U+00DF LATIN SMALL LETTER SHARP S              no     no
[k]       U+212A KELVIN SIGN                             no     yes
[a-z]     U+212A KELVIN SIGN                             no     yes
i         U+0131 LATIN SMALL LETTER DOTLESS I            no     yes
i         U+0130 LATIN CAPITAL LETTER I WITH DOT ABOVE   no     yes
i U+0307  U+0130 LATIN CAPITAL LETTER I WITH DOT ABOVE   no     no

does (?i) fold the BACKREFERENCE comparison too?
(?i)(a)\1    vs 'a' + U+0041            yes
(?iu)(k)\1   vs 'k' + U+212A            yes

one program, two answers about the same pair
  toUpperCase(U+00DF, ROOT) is 'SS'     true     regex (?iu): no
  equalsIgnoreCase(U+00DF, 'ss')        false    regex (?iu): no
  equalsIgnoreCase(U+212A, 'k')         true     regex (?iu): yes
  toLowerCase(U+1E9E, ROOT) is U+00DF   true     regex (?iu): no

how one character is folded onto another: lower(upper(cp))
  U+00DF  toUpperCase U+00DF  then toLowerCase U+00DF
  U+1E9E  toUpperCase U+1E9E  then toLowerCase U+00DF
  U+0131  toUpperCase U+0049  then toLowerCase U+0069
  U+0130  toUpperCase U+0130  then toLowerCase U+0069

the locale the regex engine refuses to have
  toLowerCase('I', Locale.ROOT)          U+0069
  toLowerCase('I', Locale tr)            U+0131
  toUpperCase('i', Locale tr)            U+0130
  (?iu)i vs U+0131                       yes
  (?iu)I vs U+0131                       yes
